walkthrough "linux-fedora-workstation - sshd"
  resolution 1920x1080
  password admin
  var out == {default: out/distro.qcow2, from: args.out}
  var user == {default: vmuser, from: args.user}
  header recording_resolution == [1920, 1080]

  step "boot the disk"
    boot == true
    os == linux
    # A FIXED SIZE FOR EVERY GUEST OF THIS FAMILY: see the header comment.
    memory_mb == 4096
    timeout == 900
  # THE GREETER IS ASKED FOR BEFORE ANYTHING IS CLICKED, BECAUSE IT IS NOT THERE YET.
  #
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44472625613, whose recording is BLACK at 15 seconds and shows the greeter at
  # 22: the clicks and the `type:` that followed all landed on nothing, so the password field kept
  # its "Password" placeholder and `wait for the desktop` expired after 15 minutes. Every step below
  # carries `optional: true`, which is what let the miss through silently - an optional step that
  # misses is indistinguishable from one that was not needed.
  #
  # THE BAND IS THE GREETER'S OWN MIDDLE: the user tile and the password field, x 740..1340 and
  # y 470..770. Read back from that recording at scale 2 it yields "Password vmuser"; at scale 1 it
  # yields nothing, which is why the scale is 2 and not the default.
  #
  # AND IT IS *OPTIONAL*, BECAUSE THIS MEDIUM'S DISK AUTO-LOGS-IN AND NEVER SHOWS A GREETER.
  #
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44552110478, which failed here at step 2 of 23 with
  #
  #	see: 22 word(s): ... come to Fedora Linux 45 (Workstation Edition)!
  #	the greeter is drawn [check]: up = false (none of "password" or "vmuser" in ...)
  #
  # That screen is the FIRST-LOGIN WELCOME, i.e. the session is already up - exactly what the rest
  # of this file handles with `optional: true` on every greeter step. A wait that blocks when the
  # greeter is absent turns "this medium auto-logs-in" into a failure.
  # THIS DISK HAS NO GREETER, AND THE FOUR STEPS THAT PRETENDED IT MIGHT WERE DOING HARM.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THE POD, with the published `linux-fedora-workstation` disk, reading the same region
  # five times four seconds apart:
  #
  #	"seeliano En 'Opan Platform Firmware Development"     the firmware screen
  #	""                                                     black
  #	"a ill J B Q . i y ' =n q a ..."                       the boot splash
  #	"Rood ww s = come to Fedora Linux 45 (Workstation Edition)!"   THE WIZARD
  #	"Rood ww s = come to Fedora Linux 45 (Workstation Edition)!"   still the wizard
  #
  # The check asked for "password" or "vmuser" and answered false - correctly, there is no greeter
  # on this disk. AND THEN THE FOUR STEPS BELOW IT RAN ANYWAY: `optional:` makes a FAILURE non-fatal,
  # it does not stop a step from acting. So the stage pressed Escape, clicked (960,488), typed the
  # password and pressed Return - all of it into the Setup Assistant it had just found. The wizard's
  # own step later read "welcome to fedora" and passed, which is why the damage was invisible until
  # `focus the wizard's only button` tried to press Tab and the QMP connection was gone.
  #
  # A DISK THAT BOOTS TO A GREETER IS NOT WHAT THIS STAGE TESTS. This medium's install walk ends with
  # `shutdown: kill` and no account, so the first boot is GNOME Initial Setup by construction. The
  # greeter path is deleted rather than gated: a gate here would have to read the screen twice to
  # decide what it had already read once.
  # THIS DISK HAS NO USER, SO THE FIRST BOOT IS GNOME INITIAL SETUP - NOT A GREETER.
  #
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44557858241, whose ssh recording ends on the Setup window and stays there:
  #
  #	[ Setup ]
  #	Welcome to Fedora Linux 45 (Workstation Edition)!
  #	Setup will guide you through making an account and enabling some features.
  #	[ Start Setup ]
  #
  # AND THAT IS WHY THIS DISK HAS NO USER: fedora's install walk never visits anaconda's root
  # password or user page - it goes from 'next from the installation method' straight to
  # `shutdown: kill`. So the account this stage needs does not exist yet, and it has to be made
  # HERE, on the first boot, in the wizard the installer left behind.
  #
  # The other Linux media create `vmuser` inside their installer, which is why their ssh stages meet
  # a greeter. This one does not, and the greeter steps above are optional for exactly that reason.
  # FORTY ATTEMPTS WAS 200 SECONDS, AND THIS DISK TAKES MINUTES TO FINISH BOOTING.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THE POD, manually, with the published `linux-fedora-workstation` disk: step 7 ran its
  # forty attempts and the screen at the end was STILL THE BOOT SPLASH - Fedora's ring of gears on the
  # gradient background - and the check reported `read "ies"`, one fragment of a word it caught through
  # the animation. The next step then pressed Tab into a machine that was not there and the QMP
  # connection was closed by the timeout.
  #
  # A first boot of an installed Fedora regenerates the initramfs and starts GNOME Initial Setup; on a
  # cold disk that is several minutes. The budget is fifteen, and `settle: 10` between looks so the
  # forty looks are not spent describing the same splash.
  # THE WIZARD IS RECOGNISED BY ITS OWN CHROME, NOT BY WHICH PAGE IT IS SHOWING.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THE POD, with the published `linux-fedora-workstation` disk: after the boot finished
  # the wizard was ALREADY PAST the welcome page - the region this check reads held
  #
  #	"Privacy controls can be changed at any time from the Settings app."
  #
  # - the same sentence CI's log has been showing for runs, reported there as
  # `none of "welcome to fedora" or "setup will guide"`. Both were reading the wizard's SIXTH page
  # and asking for its first, so the check said "no wizard" while the wizard was on screen, and the
  # loop spent its whole budget proving it.
  #
  # EVERY PAGE OF GNOME INITIAL SETUP CARRIES "Previous" AND "Next" in the header - the welcome page
  # included, where they are drawn but disabled. That pair is the wizard's chrome and nothing else on
  # this desktop wears it.
  step "the setup wizard is up"
    loop == {check: {from: wizard, mode: text, name: up, rect: [0, 150, 1920, 1080], rect_at: [1920, 1080], scale: 1, words: [[welcome, to, fedora], [setup, will, guide], [start, setup], [privacy, controls], [previous], [third, party]]}, do: [{settle: 10}], max: 90, see: {name: wizard}, until: {fact: up}}
    optional == true
  # THE CLICK AT THE BUTTON'S OWN CENTRE DID NOT TAKE, SO THE BUTTON IS REACHED BY KEYBOARD.
  #
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44557723577's successor: 'start the setup wizard [clicked (960, 844)]' in the
  # log, and the recording's frame at 200 s is the SAME welcome page - the button highlighted, the
  # wizard not started.
  #
  # `Start Setup` is the only focusable control on that page, so Tab reaches it and Return presses
  # it. A click that lands a few pixels off is a click that does nothing; a keyboard focus ring is
  # not ambiguous.
  press Tab, waiting 2s, named "focus the wizard's only button"
  press Return, waiting 15s, named "start the setup wizard, by keyboard"
  # THE WIZARD HAS MANY PAGES AND ONE BUTTON THAT MOVES FORWARD.
  #
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44557723577's successor: after Start Setup the wizard walks itself to
  #
  #	[ Previous ]   Privacy   [ Next ]
  #	Location Services   (off)
  #	Privacy controls can be changed at any time from the Settings app.
  #
  # and stops there, because nothing presses Next. Its Next is at 1420,193 on every page of this
  # wizard (the frame above shows it there), and the wizard closes when the last one is taken.
  #
  # The loop presses Next until the wizard's own heading is gone. `max: 12` is the number of pages
  # this wizard has plus slack; a page that does not advance costs one press, not the whole stage.
  # THE WIZARD IS WALKED BY KEYBOARD TO "About You", AND THAT PAGE IS FILLED IN.
  #
  # MEASURED ACROSS SIX RUNS, AND THE LAST ONE IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS:
  #
  #   eight clicks at (1420,193) inside Next   -> nothing (the pointer does not reach this wizard)
  #   eight [Tab, Return] pairs                -> the Location Services switch toggled
  #   ten bare Returns                         -> nothing
  #   twelve alt+n                             -> the switch toggled again
  #   eight [Tab, Tab, Return] triples         -> THE WIZARD MOVED: frames at 400 s show "About You"
  #                                               with Full Name and Username, and at 500 s the
  #                                               same dialog in ENTERPRISE LOGIN mode - because one
  #                                               of the triples pressed that toggle at the bottom.
  #
  # So Tab+Tab+Return is the sequence that advances pages, and on "About You" the page it lands on is
  # the account form this stage needs. The cursor is IN the Full Name field on that frame (its border
  # is the accent colour), so the fields are typed without a click.
  #
  # THE FIELDS ARE THE TWO THE FORM SHOWS - Full Name and Username - and GNOME auto-fills the user
  # name from the full name as it is typed, which is why the second field is CLEARED before typing
  # (the fault that made manjaro's account `vmuservmuser`). Next enables once both are valid; the
  # password is asked for on the page after it.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THE POD, AND THE FIXED COUNT IS WHAT THIS REPLACES. The list of eight strokes below
  # was measured across six CI runs, and on this disk it stopped the wizard on
  #
  #	[ Previous ]   Third-Party Repositories   [ Next ]
  #	Third-party repositories provide access to additional software ...
  #	[ Disable Third-Party Repositories ]
  #
  # - TWO PAGES SHORT of the account form, with the whole rest of the stage waiting behind it. A
  # fixed number of presses encodes "this image has N pages before About You", which is a fact about
  # one image rather than about the wizard. THE NUMBER OF PAGES IS NOT THIS STAGE'S BUSINESS; the
  # page it has to reach is.
  #
  # SO THE SAME TRIPLE IS PRESSED UNTIL THE ACCOUNT FORM IS UP, recognised by the same picture the
  # next step judged it by. `max: 8` is every page this wizard has plus slack - a page that refuses
  # to advance costs one triple, not the stage.
  #
  # THE TRIPLE ITSELF IS MEASURED AND STAYS: Tab reaches the switch, Tab reaches Next, Return presses
  # it. A bare click at Next does nothing on this wizard, and a bare Return does nothing either -
  # both were tried and both were recorded (see the note above).
  # THE ACCOUNT FORM IS RECOGNISED BY A PICTURE, NOT BY WORDS OR A GUESS ABOUT FOCUS.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THIS WIZARD, six runs: the words on the page are not unique to it (Privacy, the update
  # pane and this form are all white cards with a title and a blue Next), and the two ways it can look
  # are "Create Account" (Full Name + Username) and "Enterprise Login" (Domain + Username + Password).
  # A stage that typed on the wrong one put the password into a domain field and the wizard sat there.
  #
  # SO THE REGION IS COMPARED AS A PICTURE against a frame from run 145ff2b, in which the cursor is IN
  # the Full Name field - which is the state this stage needs, not merely a page that mentions the
  # same words.
  #
  # THE NUMBERS ARE MEASURED WITH `tools/reflike.py`, WHICH USES THE ENGINE'S OWN RULE.
  #
  # `reflike.py suggest` SEARCHES for the rectangle that separates the state from everything else,
  # rather than taking the one I drew by eye - and it found a better one:
  #
  #	rect [700, 300, 1100, 420]   match 1.0000   worst reject 0.7551   gap 0.2449
  #
  # Against the box I had chosen first, [710, 530, 1215, 655], whose gap was 0.111:
  #
  #	1.0000  create-account form, cursor in Full Name     <- the state to act on
  #	0.8890  an earlier wizard page                       <- only 0.11 below
  #
  # The difference is what is INSIDE the box. The first one framed the two input fields, which are
  # white on both this page and every other page of the wizard; the second frames the row of marks
  # above them - the account silhouette, the "About You" heading and its one line of body text - which
  # exists on this page and nowhere else.
  #
  # `range: [0.78, 1.00]` is the tool's suggestion: the worst reject plus a 0.02 margin, so ordinary
  # rendering jitter cannot flip it.
  #
  # THE ACCOUNT FORM IS RECOGNISED BY A PICTURE, NOT BY WORDS OR A GUESS ABOUT FOCUS.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THIS WIZARD, six runs: the words on the page are not unique to it (Privacy, the update
  # pane and this form are all white cards with a title and a blue Next), and the two ways it can look
  # are "Create Account" (Full Name + Username) and "Enterprise Login" (Domain + Username + Password).
  # A stage that typed on the wrong one put the password into a domain field and the wizard sat there.
  #
  # SO THE REGION IS COMPARED AS A PICTURE against a frame from run 145ff2b, in which the cursor is IN
  # the Full Name field - which is the state this stage needs, not merely a page that mentions the
  # same words.
  #
  # THE NUMBERS ARE MEASURED WITH `tools/reflike.py`, WHICH USES THE ENGINE'S OWN RULE.
  #
  # `reflike.py suggest` SEARCHES for the rectangle that separates the state from everything else,
  # rather than taking the one I drew by eye - and it found a better one:
  #
  #	rect [700, 300, 1100, 420]   match 1.0000   worst reject 0.7551   gap 0.2449
  #
  # Against the box I had chosen first, [710, 530, 1215, 655], whose gap was 0.111:
  #
  #	1.0000  create-account form, cursor in Full Name     <- the state to act on
  #	0.8890  an earlier wizard page                       <- only 0.11 below
  #
  # The difference is what is INSIDE the box. The first one framed the two input fields, which are
  # white on both this page and every other page of the wizard; the second frames the row of marks
  # above them - the account silhouette, the "About You" heading and its one line of body text - which
  # exists on this page and nowhere else.
  #
  # `range: [0.78, 1.00]` is the tool's suggestion: the worst reject plus a 0.02 margin, so ordinary
  # rendering jitter cannot flip it.
  #
  # EVERY STROKE BELOW WAS MEASURED ON THE POD THROUGH THE DAEMON'S OWN HTTP ENDPOINT, PAGE BY PAGE,
  # AND THE WIZARD WAS WALKED TO "All done!" THAT WAY. What it replaces was a fixed list of
  # [Tab, Tab, Return] triples that stopped on "Third-Party Repositories" - and a `loop:` that pressed
  # the same triple, which stopped on the same page for the same reason.
  #
  # THE TRIPLE WAS WRONG ABOUT THIS WIZARD. Tab from a page's content moves BACKWARDS to Previous, so
  # pressing Tab twice and then Return put the wizard on the page before the one it had reached. The
  # measurements, each confirmed by reading the frame afterwards:
  #
  #	Welcome      Return alone            -> Privacy
  #	Privacy      Shift+Tab, Return       -> Time Zone
  #	Time Zone    (see the map below)     -> Third-Party Repositories
  #	Third-Party  Return alone            -> About You
  #
  # Return ALONE is what works on most pages, because the wizard focuses Next when a page opens. The
  # pages where it does not are the two whose content takes the focus - Privacy's switch and Time
  # Zone's search box - and there Shift+Tab is what reaches Next.
  # EVERY PAGE AFTER THE WELCOME IS LEFT BY CLICKING NEXT, AND THAT WAS MEASURED.
  #
  # THIS REPLACES THREE EARLIER ATTEMPTS AT THE SAME ROUTE, AND ALL THREE FAILED THE SAME WAY - by
  # pressing keys at a wizard whose focus they did not know:
  #
  #	fixed [Tab, Tab, Return] x8          stopped on "Third-Party Repositories"
  #	Tab,Tab,Return in a `loop:`          stopped on the same page
  #	Return / Shift+Tab+Return per page    left the screen ON PRIVACY (OCR read "Previous Privacy
  #	                                      Privacy") while the next four steps walked a wizard that
  #	                                      had not moved
  #
  # The last one is the dangerous shape: a keystroke on the wrong page is still a keystroke, so every
  # later step reported a real action against the wrong screen. On the pod, the same route by
  # CLICKING went from Privacy to Third-Party on the first click and to About You on the second, and
  # Previous walked it back the same way. The pointer is the part of this wizard that behaves.
  #
  # THE WELCOME PAGE IS HANDLED ABOVE, by `Tab, Return` on "Start Setup": it is the only control on
  # that page, so there is nothing else for the focus to land on.
  #
  # THE PRIVACY PAGE IS LEFT THE SAME WAY - one click on Next, at (1413, 193), the coordinate the
  # wizard draws it at on every page. Its location switch is left alone: this stage is not about
  # location services, and clicking the page's centre would toggle it.
  step "leave the privacy page"
    click == [1413, 193]
    settle == 5
  #
  # (640, 690) IS A LANDMARK, NOT A NAVIGATION. It is Los Angeles on the map, and clicking it sets a
  # time zone worth having - confirmed by the page's own bubble reading
  # "PST (UTC-08) Los Angeles, United States". It is NOT what makes the page pass: measured, Next on
  # an unanswered Time Zone page is the same blue button and moves the wizard on to Third-Party.
  step "point the map at Los Angeles, when the wizard is showing one"
    click == [640, 690]
    settle == 3
  step "leave the time zone page"
    click == [1413, 193]
    settle == 5
  step "leave the third-party repositories page"
    click == [1413, 193]
    settle == 5
  # THE ACCOUNT FORM IS RECOGNISED BY A PICTURE, NOT BY WORDS OR A GUESS ABOUT FOCUS.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THIS WIZARD, six runs: the words on the page are not unique to it (Privacy, the update
  # pane and this form are all white cards with a title and a blue Next), and the two ways it can look
  # are "Create Account" (Full Name + Username) and "Enterprise Login" (Domain + Username + Password).
  # A stage that typed on the wrong one put the password into a domain field and the wizard sat there.
  #
  # SO THE REGION IS COMPARED AS A PICTURE against a frame in which the cursor is IN the Full Name
  # field - which is the state this stage needs, not merely a page that mentions the same words.
  #
  # `rect [700, 300, 1100, 420]` IS FROM `reflike.py suggest`, and it frames the row of marks above
  # the fields - the account silhouette, the "About You" heading and its one line of body text -
  # which exists on this page and nowhere else. The box around the input fields scored worse because
  # white fields look the same on every page of the wizard.
  #
  # MEASURED AGAINST THE REJECT THAT MATTERS: the Third-Party page scores 0.7093 here, below the
  # 0.78 floor, so arriving on the wrong page cannot be mistaken for arriving on this one.
  #
  # THIS STEP IS NOT OPTIONAL. The four pages above are a route; this is the destination, and a route
  # that ends anywhere else has failed.
  step "look at the page the route ended on"
    see == {name: account_form}
    settle == 2
  step "the create-account form is up, with the cursor in Full Name"
    check == {from: account_form, mode: like, name: ready, rect: [700, 300, 1100, 420], rect_at: [1920, 1080], reference: reference/about-you-create-account.png, range: [0.78, 1.0], must: true}
  # THE FIELDS ARE TYPED NOW THAT THE PAGE IS PROVEN, AND THE PROOF IS ABOVE RATHER THAN A `when:`.
  #
  # A `when: [ready]` GATE WAS TRIED AND REJECTED: `doCheck` only binds the fact when the check RUNS,
  # and inside an `optional` loop a page that never matched left the name unbound and the gate skipped
  # the typing silently - the same "optional step that let the miss through" fault this file's header
  # complains about. A non-optional check has none of that ambiguity: it fails, or it proves.
  step "the full name"
    type == '{{ user }}'
    type_gap == 0.09
    settle == 3
  press Tab, waiting 2s, named "on to the user name"
  step "clear the auto-filled user name"
    key == ctrl+a
    settle == 1
  step "the user name"
    type == '{{ user }}'
    type_gap == 0.09
    settle == 3
  press Return, waiting 10s, named "submit the account page"
  # THE PASSWORD PAGE HAS TWO FIELDS AND TAB DOES NOT REACH THE SECOND ONE.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THE POD: after typing the password and pressing Tab, the confirmation field was still
  # empty - the focus had not moved into it. Clicking it at (960, 627) put the cursor there and the
  # password was accepted, so the click is what this does. The two fields then match, Next enables, and
  # the wizard finishes on "All done!".
  step "the password"
    type == '{{ password }}'
    type_gap == 0.09
    settle == 2
  step "put the cursor in the confirmation field, which Tab does not reach"
    click == [960, 627]
    settle == 1
  step "the password, again"
    type == '{{ password }}'
    type_gap == 0.09
    settle == 2
  step "leave the password page"
    click == [1413, 193]
    settle == 4
  press Return, waiting 20s, named "finish the wizard"
  # THE TERMINAL IS OPENED BY SEARCHING IN THE OVERVIEW, AND THE WAY IN IS THE ESCAPE KEY, NOT meta_l.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THE POD, AND THIS IS WHY EVERY COMMAND AFTER IT WAS DEAD. The previous version opened
  # the overview with `meta_l`, typed "terminal" and pressed Return. The screen afterwards was
  #
  #	[ Type to search ]                                        <- the overview, NOT a terminal
  #	--reloadsudo firewall-cmd --list-services                 <- this stage's own commands, in the
  #	No results                                                   SEARCH BOX
  #
  # - every command the stage believed it was typing into a shell had gone into GNOME's search
  # field, where "No results" was the only answer it ever gave. That is why `wait for sshd` then
  # waited its full ten minutes against a guest where openssh had never been installed: nothing was
  # installed, and nothing said so.
  #
  # WHAT DOES WORK, measured: ESCAPE closes the overview, and `super`'s search then opens with an
  # empty, focused box - type "terminal", Return, and the window appears as
  #
  #	vmuser@fedora:~$
  #
  # `meta_l` LEAVES THE PREVIOUS CONTENT IN THE BOX, which is the whole difference: the wizard's last
  # keystrokes were still in it, so Return ran a search for a command line instead of starting a
  # program.
  step "start from a clean desktop"
    key == esc
    settle == 2
  press meta_l, waiting 3s, named "open the overview"
  # THE SEARCH BOX IS CLICKED BEFORE ANYTHING IS TYPED INTO IT, AND `meta_l` ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THE POD: `meta_l` draws the overview with its search field showing "Type to search" -
  # and NOT focused. The eight characters typed after it went to the DESKTOP, leaving the overview up
  # and the field empty:
  #
  #	=== search for the terminal
  #	  typed 8 character(s)
  #	=== the Terminal result has been drawn
  #	  see: 1 word(s): a                      <- the results rectangle held a fragment of the desktop
  #
  # A click at (960, 59) - the field's own centre - puts the cursor in it, and the same eight
  # characters then produced the Terminal tile immediately. The coordinate is the field's, measured
  # from a frame of this screen, not a guess about where a search box usually sits.
  step "put the cursor in the overview's search field"
    click == [960, 59]
    settle == 2
  step "search for the terminal"
    type == terminal
    type_gap == 0.08
  # THE RESULT IS WAITED FOR, THEN CLICKED, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS MEASURED.
  #
  # MEASURED ON THE POD in both directions:
  #
  #	click at (960, 178) with "terminal" drawn      -> the window opened, prompt `vmuser@fedora:~$`
  #	click at (960, 178) four seconds after typing  -> a clean desktop, NO window
  #
  # The second is what this stage did on its next run, and it is not a wrong coordinate: the search
  # result had not been drawn yet when the pointer arrived. A fixed `settle:` is a guess about how
  # long GNOME's search takes, and on a guest still finishing its first login that guess was short.
  #
  # SO THE TILE IS WAITED FOR, BY ITS OWN WORD. A search that has produced nothing draws no word in
  # that row; one that has produced the app draws "Terminal". The loop presses nothing - a key here
  # would go into the search box and change what is being searched for.
  step "the Terminal result has been drawn"
    # THE RECT IS THE TILE AND ITS LABEL, NOT THE WHOLE RESULT AREA. MEASURED with tesseract on a
    # frame of this screen: the wider box `[700, 90, 1250, 240]` returns NOTHING - the tile's icon is
    # a gradient and the text is small against it - while `[860, 150, 1060, 250]` reads "Terminal".
    # A gate whose rectangle cannot see the word it is waiting for fails for ever, quietly.
    loop == {check: {from: results, mode: text, name: drawn, rect: [860, 150, 1060, 250], rect_at: [1920, 1080], scale: 1, words: [[terminal]]}, do: [{settle: 2}], max: 10, see: {name: results}, until: {fact: drawn}}
  step "open the terminal on the first result"
    click == [960, 178]
    settle == 20
  # THE TERMINAL HAS TO BE PROVEN OPEN BEFORE ANY COMMAND IS TYPED INTO IT.
  #
  # THIS STEP WOULD HAVE CAUGHT THE FAULT ABOVE ON ITS FIRST RUN. The stage typed eight commands
  # into GNOME's search box and reported each one "running"; only `wait for sshd`, ten minutes
  # later, noticed that nothing had happened.
  #
  # The prompt is the evidence: a shell on this guest draws
  #
  #	vmuser@fedora:~$
  #
  # and GNOME's overview, which is what the older version was actually looking at, draws
  # "Type to search" instead. `[300, 280, 1300, 800]` covers the window's text area and not the
  # panel, so a title bar saying "fedora" cannot satisfy it on its own.
  #
  # THE LOOP ONLY LOOKS. Its `do:` is a `settle:` and nothing else: the window takes a few seconds to
  # map, and a loop that PRESSED a key to "help" would be pressing it into whatever had the focus -
  # which is exactly how the commands ended up in the search box.
  step "the terminal is open, with a prompt"
    loop == {check: {from: shell, mode: text, name: ready, rect: [300, 280, 1330, 810], rect_at: [1920, 1080], scale: 1, words: [[vmuser@fedora]]}, do: [{settle: 4}], max: 4, see: {name: shell}, until: {fact: ready}}
  # NO `terminal_wait:` HERE, AND THAT IS THE FIX RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.
  #
  # `terminal_wait:` OPENS A TERMINAL (ctrl+alt+t) AND THEN TYPES. The step above has already
  # opened one, so a second ctrl+alt+t starts a NEW terminal window and the keys that follow go to
  # it - while the command already typed into the first one is never submitted.
  #
  # MEASURED ON RUN 44545555943's kubuntu ssh recording, whose terminal shows exactly that:
  #
  #	vmuser@kubuntu:~$ xset s off; xset -dpms; xset s noblank; echo SCREEN_KEPT_ONECHO ADMIN | sudo ...
  #	server does not have extension for -dpms option
  #	[sudo: authenticate] Password:
  #
  # `SCREEN_KEPT_ON` runs straight into `ECHO ADMIN` with no line break: the Return after the first
  # command went to the OTHER window. And the `xset -dpms` failure - "server does not have extension
  # for -dpms option" - is harmless; the screen is still kept awake by `s off` and `s noblank`.
  # UBUNTU'S SHAPE: unlock sudo ONCE, then plain sudo for everything.
  #
  # MEASURED ACROSS THIS SESSION: every leg that failed at 'wait for sshd' had the terminal show
  # either 'CHO: command not found' (the previous command's Return was not delivered, so 'echo
  # admin' lost its first two characters) or 'sudo: no password was provided'. Both are the same
  # fault - the password travels in the command line - and both are cured by authenticating once
  # and letting sudo keep the timestamp.
  step "unlock sudo once for this session"
    run == printf '%s\n' {{ password }} | sudo -S -v
    settle == 4
  step "install openssh-server"
    run == sudo dnf install -y openssh-server
    settle == 60
  step "allow password authentication"
    run == sudo sed -i "s/^#\?\s*PasswordAuthentication.*/PasswordAuthentication yes/" /etc/ssh/sshd_config ; sudo grep -i "^PasswordAuthentication" /etc/ssh/sshd_config
    settle == 3
  step "enable ssh at boot"
    run == sudo systemctl enable --now sshd
    settle == 8
  # FEDORA SHIPS firewalld ENABLED, AND IT DROPS 22 UNTIL THE SERVICE IS ALLOWED.
  #
  # This medium is the one distribution in this tree that starts with its firewall on by default,
  # which is why every other ssh walk could get away without a line like this. `--add-service=ssh`
  # is the standard rule and it is what the default zone publishes.
  step "open the firewall for ssh"
    run == sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ssh
    settle == 4
  step "reload the firewall"
    run == sudo firewall-cmd --reload
    settle == 5
  step "show the zone's services"
    run == sudo firewall-cmd --list-services
    settle == 3
  step "wait for sshd"
    require_ssh == true
    timeout == 600
  step "prove the kernel over ssh"
    ssh == uname -a
    timeout == 120
  step "prove the account over ssh"
    ssh == id -un; hostname
    timeout == 120
  step "ask the guest to power off"
    ssh == printf '%s\n' '{{ password }}' | sudo -S -p '' poweroff
    timeout == 300
  step "wait for the machine to stop"
    timeout == 300
    wait_until == stopped
  step "publish the disk"
    compress == '{{ out }}'
